# Documentation

This guide explains sibl in four parts. Part 1 helps you build the project and run the inspection pass on your first object file. Part 2 explains the main ideas behind the linker. Part 3 goes inside the source tree and the test suite. The API reference lists every public type and function that sibl re-exports from `src/root.zig`.

If you are new to sibl, start with Part 1.

## The four parts

### Part 1: Getting Started

Build sibl, run the test suite, and link your first object file. This part also covers the command-line flags, the output of the inspection pass, the built-in layout templates, and the library API.

[Read Part 1: Getting Started](/docs/part-1-getting-started)

### Part 2: Core Concepts

This part explains object formats, symbol resolution, section grouping, segment layout, relocations, entry points, placement rules, `--defsym`, and templates.

[Read Part 2: Core Concepts](/docs/part-2-core-concepts)

### Part 3: Inside sibl

This part maps the source modules, follows one file through the linker pipeline, and explains the test and contribution rules.

[Read Part 3: Inside sibl](/docs/part-3-internals)

### API Reference

The API reference documents every public module that sibl exposes. Each page shows the main types, the functions, and a short example.

[Read the API Reference](/docs/api-reference)

## What you need

- Zig 0.17.0
- Git, if you clone the source

sibl has no external dependencies. The whole project builds with the Zig standard library.

## Most accurate reference

This website summarizes the public API. The source code comments are the most accurate reference. To read them as HTML, clone the repository and run:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/zig-foundry/sibl.git
cd sibl
zig build-lib src/root.zig -femit-docs
```

Then open the generated `docs/index.html` in your browser.

To build this website locally, run:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/zig-foundry/sibl-website.git
cd sibl-website
zine release -f
```

The output goes to `public/`.

## Current status

Phases 1 to 9 are done and tested. sibl can parse ELF, PE/COFF, Mach-O, and static archives. It can resolve symbols, lay out sections and segments, apply relocations, resolve an entry point, use placement rules, define symbols with `--defsym`, and apply a default layout template.

Binary output is not ready yet. That work is Phase 13. For now, the `sibl` command inspects inputs and prints the planned layout. It does not write a runnable binary.

[Check the roadmap](/roadmap)
